er might instead hinge on a structural trigger — entities appearing in coordinated clinical-presentation lists versus sentences describing \"other\" related conditions.\n\nActually the discrepancies seem confined to those five cases—\"chronic meningococcemia\" and \"C7 deficiency\" apparently got consistent SpecificDisease labels from both annotators, while the presenting symptoms (fever, arthritis, cutaneous vasculitis) were gold-labeled SpecificDisease but flagged DiseaseClass by the model. Yet elsewhere gold treats similar symptom-like mentions (\"mental retardation,\" \"exudative retinal detachment\") as DiseaseClass, which looks like an inconsistency in the gold annotations themselves rather than a single clean rule.\n\nI wonder if the distinguishing factor is syntactic rather than semantic—whether the phrase appears in a \"characterized by\" or parenthetical/appositive construction (which seems to get DiseaseClass) versus a \"presenting with\" or generic reference construction (which seems to get SpecificDisease in the discrepant cases).\n\nActually all these terms share something: they're generic hypernym categories rather than specific named diseases — \"fever,\" \"arthritis,\" \"cutaneous vasculitis,\" \"chronic neisserial infection\" each cover a broad class of conditions rather than one specific disease. The model is treating them as DiseaseClass for that reason, while gold insists they're SpecificDisease, which makes me think the gold annotation convention might differ from what seems intuitively correct here.\n\nLooking at more TP examples like \"exudative retinal detachment\" and \"mental retardation\" as DiseaseClass, I see the gold's DiseaseClass actually covers generic phenotypes too, which complicates my theory. The pattern I'm testing is whether named/eponymous entities (Coats, Norrie, SLS) consistently get SpecificDisease while unnamed descriptive terms get DiseaseClass—but the discrepancy cases like \"chronic neisserial infection\" being labeled